From c9200017c5aa11d67b02f2905205bfda4a1011ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stringer <jcec_1@yahoo.co.uk>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:34:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/31] Paletted renderer: free the palette when it is nearly
 full

The paletted renderer gives each new colour one of 256 host palette entries
and never frees them (the Palette_Reset in Render_DumpDisplay is commented
out). Plus games use many of the 4096 colours (Sonic GX: 200 entries in 30 s),
so after a game or two the palette is full, new colours are dropped and drawn
in a stale entry's colour. At 224 entries the palette is now freed at the end
of a frame and the next frame takes entries for the colours it uses. Only the
palette numbering changes: the RGB output is identical on every cartridge this
affects. NO_PALGC_FIX restores the old behaviour.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/cpc/render5.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cpc/render5.c b/src/cpc/render5.c
index 5742481..5e4ada4 100644
--- a/src/cpc/render5.c
+++ b/src/cpc/render5.c
@@ -1590,9 +1590,27 @@ int Render_PaletteUsedCount(void)
 }
 #endif
 
+/* Amiga port (18 Oct 2026): the paletted renderer gives each new CPC/Plus
+ * colour one of 256 host palette entries and never frees them (the
+ * Palette_Reset below is commented out upstream). Plus games set many of the
+ * 4096 colours (Sonic GX uses 200 entries in 30 s), so after a game or two the
+ * palette is full, new colours are dropped and drawn in a stale entry's colour:
+ * corruption that grew with each game started by Power ON. When it is nearly
+ * full, the palette is freed at the end of a frame (after that frame is out),
+ * and the next frame takes entries for the colours it actually uses.
+ * NO_PALGC_FIX: never freed, as before. */
+#define PALGC_LIMIT 224
 void    Render_DumpDisplay(void)
 {
         pRender_DumpScreen();
+#ifndef NO_PALGC_FIX
+	if (Renderer_Active && BytesPerPixel == 1)   /* the paletted (8-bit) renderer */
+	{
+		int i, n = 0;
+		for (i = 0; i < 256; i++) if (CPCPalette[i].Flags & PALETTE_ENTRY_USED) n++;
+		if (n >= PALGC_LIMIT) Palette_Reset();
+	}
+#endif
 #ifdef PAL_COUNT
 	{ static int f; if (++f % 100 == 0) fprintf(stderr, "frame %d palette entries used %d\n", f, Render_PaletteUsedCount()); }
 #endif
